    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d961">

                <pb facs="0913" n="599" xml:id="pg599"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72/481</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Chargé d’Affaires</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Barclay</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">British Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-08-12">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">August 12, 1914</hi>.</date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received August 13</hi>.] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">No. 267]</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In view of the state of war now existing
                    between Great Britain and Austria-Hungary I have the honour, under instructions
                    from His Majesty’s Principal Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, to refer
                    you to my note No. 252 of the 4th instant,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d961-f1"/>
                    stating the position taken by His Majesty’s Government in respect to the arming
                    of merchant vessels in neutral countries and the responsibility of neutrals.</p>
                <p>I am to call your attention to the fact that it is known that Austria-Hungary,
                    like Germany, favours the policy of converting her merchant vessels into armed
                    ships on the high seas and that it is probable therefore that attempts will be
                    made to equip and despatch merchantmen for such conversion from the ports of the
                    United States.</p>
                <p>A similar communication is being addressed by His Majesty’s representatives to
                    practically all the neutral powers.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Colville Barclay</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
